HierarchicalModels — Many Noisy Stars, One Population
====================================================

Open index.html directly, or serve it with TeachingTools alongside. This is a
local Canvas / JavaScript lesson, with no Shiny, WebR or downloads at runtime.
Original app.R and model.R remain unchanged.

Model
-----
Fourteen true velocities are Normal(1.5, tau^2). Known individual measurement
SDs run from 0.25 to 1.7 km/s times the uncertainty multiplier. Partial pooling:
  theta_i | mu ~ Normal(mu,tau^2); x_i | theta_i ~ Normal(theta_i,s_i^2)
  mu ~ Normal(0,25), with tau KNOWN.
  V = 1 / [1/25 + sum(1/(tau^2+s_i^2))]
  m = V sum[x_i/(tau^2+s_i^2)], w_i = tau^2/(tau^2+s_i^2)
  E(theta_i|x) = w_i x_i + (1-w_i)m
  Var(theta_i|x) = tau^2 s_i^2/(tau^2+s_i^2) + (1-w_i)^2 V.
The last term propagates uncertainty in mu; this is not plug-in shrinkage.

Complete pooling fits a separate tau=0 model, even if the simulated dispersion
is nonzero. No pooling uses independent flat priors and Normal(x_i,s_i^2)
posteriors. At tau=0 partial and complete pooling agree and retain NONZERO
uncertainty in the common mean. The individual intervals are marginal 95%
credible intervals, not simultaneous intervals or future-measurement bounds.

All original controls, precise/uncertain presets and teaching content remain.
Changing only pooling preserves the exact observations. Changing dispersion
also changes simulated truths; changing noise rescales the same random errors.
Animation reuses the cached result and illustrates linear interpolation of
positions/SDs in 60 steps. Only its final intervals are claimed as posteriors;
intermediate frames are visibly labelled as illustration, not sequential
Bayesian updating.

Intentional differences
-----------------------
Astro.createRng(seed) replaces R's RNG, preserving reproducibility but not R's
bitwise draws. The page adds population-mean uncertainty, own-measurement
weights and a final per-star summary table. In no-pooling mode it explicitly
reports "Not inferred" for the shared mean rather than exposing the unused
hierarchical calculation retained internally by the R code. No hierarchy has
been fitted in that mode. All of these choices are explained in the page.

Numerical API / checks
---------------------
CommonJS require("./model.js") / browser HierarchicalModelsModel exports
defaults, normalHierarchy(x,error,scatter,priorMean=0,priorSd=5),
compute(parameters), and atPhase(result,phase). Posteriors include individual
means/variances/weights and populationMean/populationSd (null for no pooling).
Pure calls validate inputs via Astro.ValidationError.

Run from the repository root with Node 22+:
  node --test HierarchicalModels/test_model.js
  TMPDIR="$PWD/HierarchicalModels" node HierarchicalModels/test_browser.js

Tests use independently R-calibrated normal-normal posterior means/variances,
check shared-mean uncertainty and a nonzero-prior-mean analytic case, exact
zero-scatter pooling, large-scatter no-pooling limits, unchanged observations
across inference models, seeded stability and boundaries. Browser tests check
all three model choices, identical zero-scatter posteriors, interpolation
warnings and rendered intervals, plus the shared general lesson suite.

Runtime assets (no others)
-------------------------
index.html, model.js, app.js, ../TeachingTools/statistics.js,
../TeachingTools/browser.js, ../TeachingTools/browser.css,
../TeachingTools/lesson.js.
